\details{
The objective is \eqn{\log q(y \mid X, \theta)} of \code{\link[=fit_latent_gp]{fit_latent_gp()}}. Its
gradient (Rasmussen and Williams, 2006, Algorithm 5.1) has an explicit
part, with the mode \eqn{\hat f} held fixed, and an implicit part through
the dependence of \eqn{\hat f} on \eqn{\theta}:
\deqn{\frac{\partial \hat f}{\partial \theta_j} = (I + K W)^{-1}
\frac{\partial K}{\partial \theta_j} \nabla \log p(y \mid \hat f),}
which needs the third derivatives of the log likelihood
(\code{\link[=evaluate_likelihood]{evaluate_likelihood()}}). Likelihood parameters are named
\verb{likelihood.<name>}, for example \code{likelihood.variance}.

Mean coefficients estimated by maximum likelihood
(\code{estimate_coefficients("ml")}) are optimized jointly with the other
parameters, on the real line, as \verb{mean.<name>} (for example
\code{mean.intercept}), starting from the generalized linear model fitted
without the latent process. Their gradient is
\eqn{H^\top \nabla \log p(y \mid \hat f)} plus the implicit part through
\eqn{\partial \hat f / \partial \beta = (I + K W)^{-1} H}.

Each evaluation starts the Newton iterations from the previous mode when
that is better than the prior mean, which saves most of the iterations
once the optimizer takes small steps. The returned model is refitted at
the optimum from the prior mean, so it equals \code{\link[=fit_latent_gp]{fit_latent_gp()}} with the
optimized parameters.

\verb{$optimization} has the fields of \code{\link[=optimize_gp]{optimize_gp()}}, with
\code{optimize_likelihood} and \code{approximation} in place of the noise fields,
and \code{at_bound}, the parameters that ended at \code{lower} or \code{upper} (on the
natural scale) or at the limits that \code{\link[=optimize_gp]{optimize_gp()}} describes for
changepoint locations and spectral frequencies.
